            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "For each pair of curves, suggest ",
              "marks": []
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "two transformations",
              "marks": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " by which the second equation may be obtained from the first.",
              "marks": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 3,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p42-q18-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "$y=2^x$ and $y=2^{x+1}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Vertical dilation by factor $2$, or horizontal translation $1$ unit left.",
          "worked": "Vertical dilation by factor $2$: replace $y$ with $\\frac{y}{2}$.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{y}{2}&=2^x\\\\y&=2\\cdot2^x\\\\y&=2^{x+1}\\end{align*}$$\nAlternatively, replace $x$ with $x+1$, giving $y=2^{x+1}$: a horizontal translation $1$ unit left.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 34
      },
      {
        "id": "p42-q18-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "$y=2x^2$ and $y=8x^2$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Vertical dilation by factor $4$, or horizontal dilation by factor $\\frac12$.",
          "worked": "Vertical dilation by factor $4$: replace $y$ with $\\frac{y}{4}$.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{y}{4}&=2x^2\\\\y&=8x^2\\end{align*}$$\nAlternatively, a horizontal dilation by factor $\\frac12$ replaces $x$ with $\\frac{x}{(\\frac12)}=2x$.\n$$\\begin{align*}y&=2(2x)^2\\\\y&=8x^2\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 34
      },
      {
        "id": "p42-q18-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "$x^2+(y-2)^2=1$ and $x^2+(y+2)^2=1$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Reflection in the $x$-axis, or vertical translation $4$ units down.",
          "worked": "Reflection in the $x$-axis replaces $y$ with $-y$.\n$$\\begin{align*}x^2+(-y-2)^2&=1\\\\x^2+(y+2)^2&=1\\end{align*}$$\nAlternatively, translation $4$ units down replaces $y$ with $y+4$.\n$$\\begin{align*}x^2+((y+4)-2)^2&=1\\\\x^2+(y+2)^2&=1\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 34
      }
    ]
  },
